  "plain_language": "If the soil's hydrogen eaters used all of that hydrogen to build biomass, the most carbon they could fix is about 0.0576 grams per square metre per year, in a typical sample (the twin supplies the hydrogen uptake; the carbon yield is another model's number). In a sample where the ontology finds no such organism (14PRr2) the answer is exactly zero by deduction. For scale, desert biological soil crusts are reported to fix roughly 10 g C per square metre per year (context only), and CO oxidation just turns CO into CO2 without changing the atmosphere's carbon.",
